4. benchmark log 100000
  N= 100000   Chain::push_front =    88.59 ms   Bag::insert(begin) = 365973.26 ms
std::vector::insert(begin()) is so slow in large n because with _every single_ insert, the _entire_ vector has to be copied and moved one to the 'right' in memory. A Chain does not pay
this penalty because it can just change the definition of the 'front'. A vector also has to copy the entire vector thus far and reallocate somewhere else every time its size doubles; a
Chain has no such requirement.

5. Mavren is correct because a vector is more efficient for a bestiary (there is no reason why one would specifically need to push something to the front) and it provides O(1) access for
finding the desired monster. A Chain would give O(n) access and risk being very slow for large bestiaries. Howeer, a Chain is better for an event log because every single event requires
one to push something new to the front, and the act of logging itself would essentially become a CPU leak, making the game's save file permanently and continuously forever more bloated
(at least as long as the log was kept), such that a sufficiently long playthrough would eventually become unplayable due to the minutes-long lag every time something attempted to be logged.